- OTP abuse prevention
- API throttling
- suspicious activity detection
- brute-force protection
- bot detection
- IP reputation checks
- device fingerprinting
- rate limiting

Future Ready:
- fraud scoring
- anomaly detection
- adaptive security policies
9. Event Bus Hardening
The Event Bus must support:
- retry queues
- dead-letter queues
- event replay
- idempotent processing
- event versioning
- event tracing
- guaranteed delivery semantics

MVP:
- BullMQ + Redis queues

Future:
- Kafka / RabbitMQ
10. Transaction & Consistency Principles
Cross-module consistency rules:

- no distributed database transactions
- use event-driven consistency
- use compensating workflows
- all critical workflows idempotent
- audit every state transition

Examples:
- order placement
- payment confirmation
- supplier settlement
- inventory reservation
11. API Governance Standards
All module APIs must support:
- versioning
- pagination
- rate limiting
- validation
- structured errors
- idempotency keys
- audit correlation IDs

Response standards:
- consistent DTO structure
- machine-readable error codes
- trace IDs for debugging
12. Engineering Governance Principles
Every module must:
- own its data
- expose public contracts only
- avoid direct DB access to other modules
- maintain README documentation
- include test coverage
- support future extraction

No module may become:
- a God object
- a hidden shared dependency
- a tightly coupled internal utility
13. Final Enterprise Verdict
This module architecture is considered:

- enterprise-grade
- modular
- future-scalable
- microservice-ready
- workflow-safe
- governance-safe
- operationally resilient
- Bharat-scale ready

No further module-level redesign is recommended unless:
- production bottlenecks emerge
- scalability constraints appear
- workflow orchestration limitations arise